# This repo is a pi *configuration*: extensions, agents, skills, docs and the
# decisions behind them. Everything a pi session GENERATES is ignored, because
# it is machine-written, changes on every run, and would bury the config it sits
# beside. The rule of thumb: if deleting it costs nothing but a re-run, ignore it.
#
# Machine-state and secret paths are pinned to a leading `*/` (any profile
# directory: agent/, quant-trader/, the orchestrator symlink, ...), because the
# same files a profile generates must be ignored in every profile, not just the
# one this repo started with.

# ---- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The real .env: the ONLY file in a profile holding live credentials, plus the
# provider base URL. Everything agent/extensions/litellm.ts and firecrawl-cli.ts
# need is read from it — LITELLM_API_KEY and LITELLM_BASE_URL for the litellm
# provider, FIRECRAWL_API_URL for the librarian's research CLI.
# Losing this file means re-minting the provider key; losing it to git history
# would mean rotating it under pressure.
*/.env
*/.env.*
!*/.env.example
# NOTE: agent/extensions/litellm.ts used to be ignored here because it
# hardcoded a key. It now reads from a profile .env, so it is committed. This is
# the change the old comment on litellm.ts anticipated: its doc comments always
# claimed LITELLM_API_KEY came from the environment, and now that is true.
# Credential stores. Empty right now, which is exactly why they are easy to
# commit by accident and then hard to notice when they fill up.
*/auth.json
*/models-store.json
# Per-server OAuth state (registered client + tokens) from an mcp.json "oauth" entry.
# pi's built-in MCP extension keeps this in the mcp-auth.json FILE; a profile's
# mcp-auth/ directory is the old local extension's, kept ignored so a leftover
# never lands in git.
*/mcp-auth.json
*/mcp-auth/
# Connection log written by the built-in MCP extension.
*/mcp.log
# pi's project-trust decisions (absolute paths of folders you trusted). Written
# by pi's trust prompt, per machine; a cloned copy must ask again, not inherit.
*/trust.json
# Never commit a backup of a config that may contain a key.
*.bak
*.orig-backup

# ---- Personal identity ------------------------------------------------------
# Skills written for one operator, carrying institutional identifiers rather
# than placeholders. Nothing here is a credential, which is why it reads as
# harmless and is not: an identifier plus the service it belongs to is enough
# to act against its owner.
# Kept on disk so the skill still loads locally, untracked so publishing this
# repo cannot leak an identity. Placeholdering was rejected — such a file's
# value is being specific, so a placeholdered copy just gets re-personalised.
# Add a path here rather than sanitising in place. The path carries the skill's
# category, because a skill sitting loose at the top level of the library breaks
# the layout the library asserts (`lib/skill-catalogue.test.ts`) — a gitignored
# skill is still a skill, and the guard walks the live tree, not the index.
*/skills/other/uarizona-hpc/

# ---- Dependencies -----------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
# The root one is a symlink to pi's global install (scripts/setup-deps.sh);
# `node_modules/` matches directories only, not a link.
/node_modules
# bun's lockfile is binary; the text lockfile is committed instead if present.
bun.lockb

# ---- Session and transcript data --------------------------------------------
# Full conversation transcripts. Large, constantly rewritten, and they contain
# whatever the agents happened to read — including file contents from anywhere
# on this machine.
*/sessions/
*/subagent-sessions/
# subagent-herdr extension: one dir per delegated run (child session file,
# .exit sidecar, system prompt, meta.json). Machine state, never config.
*/subagent-runs/
# pi's managed install: releases/, current-version — machine state.
*/install/

# herdr's pi integration. Installed and overwritten by herdr itself (its header
# says so), so tracking it would only record herdr upgrades as diffs here.
*/extensions/herdr-agent-state.ts

# Generated Dockerfiles for the ralph-loop runtime gate, one dir per project.
# Derived from each project's manifests and rebuildable on demand — deleting them
# costs one image build.
*/verify-images/

# pi-blackhole's state directory is machine-written EXCEPT for its config file,
# which is hand-tuned configuration and belongs in git like settings.json does.
# Note the `/*` — a bare `pi-blackhole/` (the whole directory) excludes it, and
# git never descends into an excluded directory, so a negation like
# `!…/pi-blackhole-config.json` under it would be silently dead. Ignoring the
# directory's *contents* keeps the negation reachable. Everything else here
# stays ignored: per-session `<id>-pending.json` / `-pending.stale.json` run
# state and `debug.ndjson`.
*/pi-blackhole/*
!*/pi-blackhole/pi-blackhole-config.json
# Plans are per-session working state keyed by session id, and the archive grows
# without bound. The decisions worth keeping end up in docs/adr and CONTEXT.md.
/*/plans/
# Exported single-session HTML, megabytes each.
pi-session-*.html

# ---- Logs, scratch and crash output ----------------------------------------
*/run-history.jsonl
*/pi-tui-crash.log
herdr.jsonl
*.log
# Loose scratch files from debugging sessions.
g.jsonl
o3.raw

# ---- Local caches -----------------------------------------------------------
*/fff/
*/npm/
/*/bin/
*/git/
*/cache/
/*/cache/
# Scratch materialisation of the git index by scripts/check.sh (CHECK_STAGED=1).
.git-check/

# Scratch space for the librarian's research: scraped pages, and shallow clones
# of third-party source under src/ that it points the explorer at. Written by
# the `firecrawl` CLI's -o convention, never by a human, and never part of the
# repo -- a clone in here is someone else's git history, not ours.
.firecrawl/

# ---- Editor and OS noise ----------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---- Startup-panel images ---------------------------------------------------
# A profile's images/ is the user's picture folder for startup-fetch.ts (which
# one shows: settings.json startupFetch.mode/image). Personal and usually
# third-party art, so the pictures stay local; only the README is tracked so
# a fresh clone has the folder. The cycle/random state file is machine state.
/*/images/*
!/*/images/README.md

# The four rules above (plans, bin, cache, images) are machine state that lives at
# a PROFILE ROOT, so they are anchored to one path component. Two top-level trees
# in this repo are SOURCE, not profile state, and must never be swallowed by them:
# docs/ (notes, diagrams, adr) and scripts/ (a tool under scripts/bin/ is a
# committed file). plans/bin/cache are DIRECTORY patterns, so they are negated as
# directories — a negated file cannot be re-included while its parent directory is
# excluded; images is a FILE pattern, so it is negated as files.
!/docs/plans/
!/docs/bin/
!/docs/cache/
!/docs/images/**
!/scripts/plans/
!/scripts/bin/
!/scripts/cache/
!/scripts/images/**
